1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,915
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,095 --> 00:00:04,153
When people hear retire early,

3
00:00:04,153 --> 00:00:07,514
they usually picture tech millionaires or lottery

4
00:00:07,514 --> 00:00:08,063
winners.

5
00:00:08,243 --> 00:00:10,738
But some people with ordinary incomes,

6
00:00:10,738 --> 00:00:12,576
teachers, nurses, engineers,

7
00:00:12,576 --> 00:00:16,319
government workers, reach financial independence in their

8
00:00:16,319 --> 00:00:17,960
forties or early fifties.

9
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:19,348
They don't win anything.

10
00:00:19,528 --> 00:00:21,639
They use one number most people never

11
00:00:21,639 --> 00:00:22,210
calculate.

12
00:00:22,390 --> 00:00:24,158
Today I'll show you that number,

13
00:00:24,158 --> 00:00:25,153
and the full plan.

14
00:00:25,502 --> 00:00:28,056
Retiring early doesn't have to mean never

15
00:00:28,056 --> 00:00:28,927
working again.

16
00:00:29,107 --> 00:00:30,132
For most people,

17
00:00:30,132 --> 00:00:32,756
it means reaching financial independence:

18
00:00:32,756 --> 00:00:35,957
having enough invested that work becomes optional.

19
00:00:36,137 --> 00:00:38,587
You can keep working at something you

20
00:00:38,587 --> 00:00:39,977
love, work part time,

21
00:00:39,977 --> 00:00:41,102
start a business,

22
00:00:41,102 --> 00:00:42,360
or stop completely.

23
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:43,806
The choice is yours.

24
00:00:44,156 --> 00:00:45,740
This idea has a name:

25
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:49,212
FIRE, which stands for Financial Independence,

26
00:00:49,212 --> 00:00:50,193
Retire Early.

27
00:00:50,373 --> 00:00:53,407
It became popular through books like Your

28
00:00:53,407 --> 00:00:55,849
Money or Your Life by Vicki Robin

29
00:00:55,849 --> 00:00:57,181
and Joe Dominguez,

30
00:00:57,181 --> 00:00:58,957
first published in 1992,

31
00:00:58,957 --> 00:01:02,435
and later through online communities and blogs.

32
00:01:02,615 --> 00:01:05,100
And its core math is surprisingly simple.

33
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:07,818
We'll look at this from three angles.

34
00:01:07,998 --> 00:01:09,241
First, the numbers:

35
00:01:09,241 --> 00:01:11,204
how much you need and how long

36
00:01:11,204 --> 00:01:13,559
it takes, based mostly on one thing.

37
00:01:13,739 --> 00:01:15,271
Second, the psychology:

38
00:01:15,271 --> 00:01:17,003
what makes this path work,

39
00:01:17,003 --> 00:01:18,801
and what makes people quit.

40
00:01:18,981 --> 00:01:20,562
And third, the playbook:

41
00:01:20,562 --> 00:01:23,393
twelve steps, plus how to handle healthcare

42
00:01:23,393 --> 00:01:26,752
and access your money before traditional retirement

43
00:01:26,752 --> 00:01:27,015
age.

44
00:01:27,366 --> 00:01:29,316
Stay for the savings rate table.

45
00:01:29,496 --> 00:01:31,601
It might be the most important chart

46
00:01:31,601 --> 00:01:32,770
in personal finance.

47
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:34,517
Let's start with the numbers.

48
00:01:34,868 --> 00:01:37,780
The four percent rule comes from financial

49
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:40,969
planner William Bengen's 1994 research and the

50
00:01:40,969 --> 00:01:44,020
later Trinity study by professors at Trinity

51
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:44,782
University.

52
00:01:44,962 --> 00:01:47,689
Growth examples use stated math at a

53
00:01:47,689 --> 00:01:50,416
five percent return after inflation,

54
00:01:50,416 --> 00:01:52,764
an illustration, not a promise.

55
00:01:52,944 --> 00:01:56,455
Retirement account and early withdrawal rules come

56
00:01:56,455 --> 00:01:57,368
from the IRS.

57
00:01:57,548 --> 00:02:00,845
Health insurance information comes from healthcare dot

58
00:02:00,845 --> 00:02:01,089
gov.

59
00:02:01,269 --> 00:02:02,619
This is education,

60
00:02:02,619 --> 00:02:04,195
not financial advice.

61
00:02:04,545 --> 00:02:05,276
Angle one.

62
00:02:05,456 --> 00:02:06,176
The numbers.

63
00:02:06,356 --> 00:02:07,575
How much do you need,

64
00:02:07,575 --> 00:02:09,084
and how long will it take?

65
00:02:09,435 --> 00:02:10,979
Let's start with how much.

66
00:02:11,159 --> 00:02:14,739
In 1994, a financial planner named William

67
00:02:14,739 --> 00:02:17,126
Bengen studied historical U.

68
00:02:17,306 --> 00:02:17,608
S.

69
00:02:17,788 --> 00:02:19,389
market returns and asked:

70
00:02:19,389 --> 00:02:21,822
how much could a retiree withdraw each

71
00:02:21,822 --> 00:02:23,678
year, adjusted for inflation,

72
00:02:23,678 --> 00:02:26,239
without running out of money over thirty

73
00:02:26,239 --> 00:02:26,623
years?

74
00:02:26,803 --> 00:02:29,198
His answer was about four percent of

75
00:02:29,198 --> 00:02:30,727
the starting portfolio.

76
00:02:30,907 --> 00:02:33,229
That became known as the four percent

77
00:02:33,229 --> 00:02:33,543
rule.

78
00:02:33,893 --> 00:02:35,901
Flip the four percent rule around,

79
00:02:35,901 --> 00:02:37,672
and you get the rule of twenty

80
00:02:37,672 --> 00:02:37,968
five.

81
00:02:38,148 --> 00:02:41,005
Multiply your yearly spending by twenty five,

82
00:02:41,005 --> 00:02:43,164
and that's a rough estimate of the

83
00:02:43,164 --> 00:02:44,371
portfolio you need.

84
00:02:44,551 --> 00:02:46,652
Spend forty thousand dollars a year?

85
00:02:46,832 --> 00:02:49,362
Twenty five times that is one million

86
00:02:49,362 --> 00:02:49,909
dollars.

87
00:02:50,089 --> 00:02:51,540
Spend thirty thousand?

88
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:53,519
Seven hundred fifty thousand.

89
00:02:53,870 --> 00:02:56,766
Multiply yearly spending by twenty five to

90
00:02:56,766 --> 00:02:59,593
estimate a financial independence number.

91
00:02:59,773 --> 00:03:01,929
Forty thousand a year means about one

92
00:03:01,929 --> 00:03:02,862
million dollars.

93
00:03:03,212 --> 00:03:05,034
An important caveat.

94
00:03:05,214 --> 00:03:07,591
The four percent rule was built around

95
00:03:07,591 --> 00:03:09,092
thirty year retirements.

96
00:03:09,272 --> 00:03:11,179
If you retire at forty five,

97
00:03:11,179 --> 00:03:13,426
your money may need to last fifty

98
00:03:13,426 --> 00:03:13,835
years.

99
00:03:14,015 --> 00:03:16,628
Many early retirees use a more cautious

100
00:03:16,628 --> 00:03:19,174
withdrawal rate, like three and a half

101
00:03:19,174 --> 00:03:21,988
percent, which means a portfolio closer to

102
00:03:21,988 --> 00:03:24,533
twenty eight or thirty times spending.

103
00:03:24,713 --> 00:03:25,921
We'll keep that in mind.

104
00:03:26,271 --> 00:03:27,908
Now the big insight.

105
00:03:28,088 --> 00:03:30,046
Notice that your FI number depends on

106
00:03:30,046 --> 00:03:31,687
your spending, not your income.

107
00:03:31,867 --> 00:03:33,344
And the time it takes to get

108
00:03:33,344 --> 00:03:35,561
there depends mostly on your savings rate:

109
00:03:35,561 --> 00:03:37,197
the share of your take home pay

110
00:03:37,197 --> 00:03:37,672
you save.

111
00:03:37,852 --> 00:03:38,908
Not your salary.

112
00:03:39,088 --> 00:03:40,330
Your savings rate.

113
00:03:40,681 --> 00:03:41,458
Here's why.

114
00:03:41,638 --> 00:03:42,825
When you save more,

115
00:03:42,825 --> 00:03:44,448
two things happen at once.

116
00:03:44,628 --> 00:03:46,126
You invest more each year.

117
00:03:46,306 --> 00:03:48,269
And you train yourself to live on

118
00:03:48,269 --> 00:03:50,590
less, which lowers the amount you need.

119
00:03:50,770 --> 00:03:52,699
Both sides of the equation move in

120
00:03:52,699 --> 00:03:54,288
your favor at the same time.

121
00:03:54,638 --> 00:03:55,764
So here's the table.

122
00:03:55,944 --> 00:03:57,782
Assuming you start from zero,

123
00:03:57,782 --> 00:04:00,381
earn five percent a year after inflation,

124
00:04:00,381 --> 00:04:02,979
and need twenty five times your spending.

125
00:04:03,159 --> 00:04:05,242
Save ten percent of your take home

126
00:04:05,242 --> 00:04:07,264
pay, and it takes about fifty two

127
00:04:07,264 --> 00:04:09,591
years to reach financial independence.

128
00:04:09,771 --> 00:04:11,190
Save twenty percent,

129
00:04:11,190 --> 00:04:12,964
about thirty seven years.

130
00:04:13,144 --> 00:04:14,408
Save thirty percent,

131
00:04:14,408 --> 00:04:15,989
about twenty eight years.

132
00:04:16,339 --> 00:04:17,494
Save forty percent,

133
00:04:17,494 --> 00:04:19,682
and it takes about twenty two years.

134
00:04:19,862 --> 00:04:21,326
Save fifty percent,

135
00:04:21,326 --> 00:04:23,020
about seventeen years.

136
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:24,617
Save sixty percent,

137
00:04:24,617 --> 00:04:26,184
about thirteen years.

138
00:04:26,364 --> 00:04:28,891
Your salary doesn't appear anywhere in that

139
00:04:28,891 --> 00:04:29,243
table.

140
00:04:29,423 --> 00:04:32,599
Someone earning fifty thousand and someone earning

141
00:04:32,599 --> 00:04:33,932
two hundred thousand,

142
00:04:33,932 --> 00:04:35,647
with the same savings rate,

143
00:04:35,647 --> 00:04:37,933
need about the same number of years.

144
00:04:38,284 --> 00:04:41,174
Years to financial independence from zero,

145
00:04:41,174 --> 00:04:42,276
by savings rate,

146
00:04:42,276 --> 00:04:45,029
at a five percent return after inflation

147
00:04:45,029 --> 00:04:47,300
and a target of twenty five times

148
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:47,920
spending.

149
00:04:48,270 --> 00:04:49,198
Let's apply it.

150
00:04:49,378 --> 00:04:52,151
A household brings home fifty five hundred

151
00:04:52,151 --> 00:04:53,999
dollars a month after taxes.

152
00:04:54,179 --> 00:04:56,536
They spend thirty six hundred and save

153
00:04:56,536 --> 00:04:59,264
nineteen hundred, about thirty five percent.

154
00:04:59,444 --> 00:05:02,005
Their yearly spending is about forty three

155
00:05:02,005 --> 00:05:04,383
thousand dollars, so their FI number is

156
00:05:04,383 --> 00:05:06,457
around one point oh eight million.

157
00:05:06,807 --> 00:05:09,540
Investing nineteen hundred dollars a month at

158
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:11,302
five percent after inflation,

159
00:05:11,302 --> 00:05:13,550
they could reach that number in about

160
00:05:13,550 --> 00:05:14,643
twenty four years.

161
00:05:14,823 --> 00:05:15,809
Start at thirty,

162
00:05:15,809 --> 00:05:18,766
and they could be financially independent around

163
00:05:18,766 --> 00:05:19,444
fifty four.

164
00:05:19,624 --> 00:05:21,137
Start at twenty five,

165
00:05:21,137 --> 00:05:22,434
around forty nine.

166
00:05:22,614 --> 00:05:24,077
On a normal income.

167
00:05:24,257 --> 00:05:26,683
With no inheritance and no lottery.

168
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:29,937
Nineteen hundred dollars a month invested at

169
00:05:29,937 --> 00:05:32,314
five percent a year after inflation,

170
00:05:32,314 --> 00:05:33,766
for twenty four years.

171
00:05:34,117 --> 00:05:35,521
And there are middle paths.

172
00:05:35,701 --> 00:05:38,301
Coast FI means saving enough early that,

173
00:05:38,301 --> 00:05:40,250
even if you stop contributing,

174
00:05:40,250 --> 00:05:42,654
your investments could grow to a full

175
00:05:42,654 --> 00:05:44,734
retirement by a traditional age.

176
00:05:44,914 --> 00:05:47,164
After that, you only need to cover

177
00:05:47,164 --> 00:05:49,943
current expenses, which lets you work less

178
00:05:49,943 --> 00:05:51,729
or take a lower stress job.

179
00:05:51,909 --> 00:05:54,152
Barista FI means covering part of your

180
00:05:54,152 --> 00:05:56,689
expenses from investments and the rest from

181
00:05:56,689 --> 00:05:57,575
part time work.

182
00:05:57,925 --> 00:05:59,776
So the math works on a normal

183
00:05:59,776 --> 00:06:00,223
income.

184
00:06:00,403 --> 00:06:02,540
But it requires a high savings rate.

185
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:05,030
And that's where psychology comes in.

186
00:06:05,210 --> 00:06:06,104
Angle two.

187
00:06:06,454 --> 00:06:07,498
Angle two.

188
00:06:07,678 --> 00:06:08,770
The psychology.

189
00:06:08,950 --> 00:06:12,084
Six mindset shifts behind early retirement.

190
00:06:12,435 --> 00:06:13,294
Shift one.

191
00:06:13,474 --> 00:06:14,515
From money as stuff,

192
00:06:14,515 --> 00:06:15,401
to money as time.

193
00:06:15,581 --> 00:06:18,137
Your Money or Your Life suggested a

194
00:06:18,137 --> 00:06:21,862
powerful exercise: calculate your real hourly wage,

195
00:06:21,862 --> 00:06:24,637
after taxes, commuting and work costs.

196
00:06:24,817 --> 00:06:27,076
Then think of every purchase in hours

197
00:06:27,076 --> 00:06:27,870
of your life.

198
00:06:28,050 --> 00:06:30,263
A two thousand dollar upgrade might be

199
00:06:30,263 --> 00:06:31,487
eighty hours of work.

200
00:06:31,667 --> 00:06:32,421
Is it worth it?

201
00:06:32,772 --> 00:06:33,909
Shift two.

202
00:06:34,089 --> 00:06:36,527
From deprivation, to optimization.

203
00:06:36,707 --> 00:06:40,260
Successful early retirees usually don't feel deprived.

204
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:42,676
They cut ruthlessly on things they don't

205
00:06:42,676 --> 00:06:44,799
care about and spend happily on things

206
00:06:44,799 --> 00:06:45,247
they do.

207
00:06:45,427 --> 00:06:47,183
One might drive an old car but

208
00:06:47,183 --> 00:06:48,236
travel every year.

209
00:06:48,416 --> 00:06:50,531
Another might cook at home but never

210
00:06:50,531 --> 00:06:51,412
skip a concert.

211
00:06:51,762 --> 00:06:52,934
Shift three.

212
00:06:53,114 --> 00:06:55,169
From normal, to intentional.

213
00:06:55,349 --> 00:06:58,502
Normal spending in America often includes a

214
00:06:58,502 --> 00:06:59,822
large car payment,

215
00:06:59,822 --> 00:07:00,702
a big house,

216
00:07:00,702 --> 00:07:02,315
and constant upgrades.

217
00:07:02,495 --> 00:07:05,641
Early retirement requires stepping away from normal.

218
00:07:05,821 --> 00:07:07,544
That can feel uncomfortable,

219
00:07:07,544 --> 00:07:10,744
especially when friends and family don't understand.

220
00:07:10,924 --> 00:07:12,631
Knowing your why makes it easier.

221
00:07:12,981 --> 00:07:13,886
Shift four.

222
00:07:14,066 --> 00:07:15,310
From retire from,

223
00:07:15,310 --> 00:07:16,260
to retire to.

224
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:18,515
People who plan only to escape a

225
00:07:18,515 --> 00:07:20,655
job often struggle after leaving.

226
00:07:20,835 --> 00:07:23,319
Purpose, community and routine matter.

227
00:07:23,499 --> 00:07:26,453
Many early retirees end up doing meaningful

228
00:07:26,453 --> 00:07:27,621
work they choose,

229
00:07:27,621 --> 00:07:30,094
volunteering, or building something.

230
00:07:30,274 --> 00:07:32,073
Plan what you're retiring to.

231
00:07:32,424 --> 00:07:33,329
Shift five.

232
00:07:33,509 --> 00:07:35,120
From all or nothing,

233
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:36,087
to progress.

234
00:07:36,267 --> 00:07:38,152
You don't need to save sixty percent

235
00:07:38,152 --> 00:07:38,728
to benefit.

236
00:07:38,908 --> 00:07:40,983
Going from ten to twenty five percent

237
00:07:40,983 --> 00:07:43,227
cuts roughly twenty years off the table.

238
00:07:43,407 --> 00:07:45,661
Every step up in savings rate buys

239
00:07:45,661 --> 00:07:48,048
you freedom, even if you never fully

240
00:07:48,048 --> 00:07:48,910
retire early.

241
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:50,630
And shift six.

242
00:07:50,810 --> 00:07:52,783
From someday, to F you money.

243
00:07:52,963 --> 00:07:55,191
Even before full independence,

244
00:07:55,191 --> 00:07:57,567
having a year or two of expenses

245
00:07:57,567 --> 00:07:59,349
saved changes your life.

246
00:07:59,529 --> 00:08:01,274
You can leave a toxic job,

247
00:08:01,274 --> 00:08:03,622
negotiate harder, or take a risk on

248
00:08:03,622 --> 00:08:05,032
a better opportunity.

249
00:08:05,212 --> 00:08:08,021
Freedom starts long before the finish line.

250
00:08:08,372 --> 00:08:12,005
There's another psychological benefit people rarely mention.

251
00:08:12,185 --> 00:08:14,176
Once you know your FI number and

252
00:08:14,176 --> 00:08:17,037
your timeline, money stops feeling endless and

253
00:08:17,037 --> 00:08:17,410
vague.

254
00:08:17,590 --> 00:08:19,076
You know what enough looks like.

255
00:08:19,256 --> 00:08:21,504
Many people say that simply having a

256
00:08:21,504 --> 00:08:24,126
finish line reduced their anxiety and made

257
00:08:24,126 --> 00:08:26,624
everyday spending decisions much easier,

258
00:08:26,624 --> 00:08:29,184
because every choice connected to a clear

259
00:08:29,184 --> 00:08:29,496
goal.

260
00:08:29,846 --> 00:08:31,645
And it changes how you see your

261
00:08:31,645 --> 00:08:31,877
job.

262
00:08:32,057 --> 00:08:33,679
Instead of feeling trapped,

263
00:08:33,679 --> 00:08:35,780
you can see each paycheck as buying

264
00:08:35,780 --> 00:08:37,642
a piece of your future freedom.

265
00:08:37,822 --> 00:08:39,999
Some people say their work became more

266
00:08:39,999 --> 00:08:41,947
enjoyable once they knew it had an

267
00:08:41,947 --> 00:08:44,010
end date they had chosen themselves.

268
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:46,523
Let's be honest about why many people

269
00:08:46,523 --> 00:08:47,576
who try this quit.

270
00:08:47,756 --> 00:08:50,262
High savings rates require trade offs,

271
00:08:50,262 --> 00:08:52,109
and social pressure is real.

272
00:08:52,289 --> 00:08:54,223
Life events, like children,

273
00:08:54,223 --> 00:08:56,944
health problems or caring for parents,

274
00:08:56,944 --> 00:08:58,234
can slow progress.

275
00:08:58,414 --> 00:09:00,294
And market crashes can make the goal

276
00:09:00,294 --> 00:09:01,862
feel further away for a while.

277
00:09:02,212 --> 00:09:05,402
The people who succeed usually build flexibility

278
00:09:05,402 --> 00:09:06,333
into the plan.

279
00:09:06,513 --> 00:09:08,173
They don't need a perfect straight line.

280
00:09:08,353 --> 00:09:11,151
They accept slower years and faster years.

281
00:09:11,331 --> 00:09:13,136
They keep the big picture:

282
00:09:13,136 --> 00:09:15,982
a steadily rising savings rate and steady

283
00:09:15,982 --> 00:09:17,856
investing, over many years.

284
00:09:18,206 --> 00:09:20,541
The idea of retiring early by living

285
00:09:20,541 --> 00:09:22,292
below your means isn't new.

286
00:09:22,472 --> 00:09:24,449
But it took a clear form in

287
00:09:24,449 --> 00:09:27,378
1992, when Vicki Robin and Joe Dominguez

288
00:09:27,378 --> 00:09:29,868
published Your Money or Your Life.

289
00:09:30,048 --> 00:09:32,237
Dominguez had left Wall Street in his

290
00:09:32,237 --> 00:09:34,485
early thirties and lived on the income

291
00:09:34,485 --> 00:09:36,259
from conservative investments.

292
00:09:36,439 --> 00:09:38,281
The book asked a simple question:

293
00:09:38,281 --> 00:09:40,011
is the money you earn worth the

294
00:09:40,011 --> 00:09:41,629
life energy you trade for it?

295
00:09:41,979 --> 00:09:46,365
In 1994, William Bengen published his withdrawal

296
00:09:46,365 --> 00:09:47,644
rate research.

297
00:09:47,824 --> 00:09:51,598
In 1998, three professors at Trinity University

298
00:09:51,598 --> 00:09:55,371
tested similar ideas with different portfolios.

299
00:09:55,551 --> 00:09:58,319
Together, these studies gave people a practical

300
00:09:58,319 --> 00:10:00,439
way to estimate how much was enough.

301
00:10:00,789 --> 00:10:03,057
Then in the 2000s and 2010s,

302
00:10:03,057 --> 00:10:06,216
blogs and online forums spread the idea

303
00:10:06,216 --> 00:10:07,836
to a new generation.

304
00:10:08,016 --> 00:10:10,810
Many bloggers showed their real budgets and

305
00:10:10,810 --> 00:10:11,720
savings rates.

306
00:10:11,900 --> 00:10:13,699
Some had high incomes.

307
00:10:13,879 --> 00:10:15,586
Many had ordinary ones.

308
00:10:15,766 --> 00:10:18,623
The common thread was always the same:

309
00:10:18,623 --> 00:10:20,127
a high savings rate,

310
00:10:20,127 --> 00:10:21,556
low cost investing,

311
00:10:21,556 --> 00:10:22,232
and time.

312
00:10:22,583 --> 00:10:24,963
Today, FIRE has many flavors.

313
00:10:25,143 --> 00:10:27,158
Lean FIRE, for people who want a

314
00:10:27,158 --> 00:10:28,103
minimal budget.

315
00:10:28,283 --> 00:10:30,226
Fat FIRE, for those who want a

316
00:10:30,226 --> 00:10:31,197
larger cushion.

317
00:10:31,377 --> 00:10:32,931
Coast FI and barista FI,

318
00:10:32,931 --> 00:10:35,197
for people who want a gentler path.

319
00:10:35,377 --> 00:10:36,677
The math is the same.

320
00:10:36,857 --> 00:10:38,761
Only the spending number changes.

321
00:10:39,112 --> 00:10:41,325
Let's try the most famous exercise from

322
00:10:41,325 --> 00:10:42,688
Your Money or Your Life.

323
00:10:42,868 --> 00:10:44,667
Start with your take home pay.

324
00:10:44,847 --> 00:10:47,494
Then subtract the costs of working:

325
00:10:47,494 --> 00:10:49,309
commuting, work clothes,

326
00:10:49,309 --> 00:10:52,259
lunches out, the extra takeout on tired

327
00:10:52,259 --> 00:10:52,788
nights.

328
00:10:52,968 --> 00:10:54,918
Then add up all the hours your

329
00:10:54,918 --> 00:10:56,023
job really takes,

330
00:10:56,023 --> 00:10:58,622
including the commute and recovery time.

331
00:10:58,973 --> 00:11:01,469
Say you take home four thousand dollars

332
00:11:01,469 --> 00:11:03,582
a month and work forty five hours

333
00:11:03,582 --> 00:11:05,950
a week, plus five hours of commuting.

334
00:11:06,130 --> 00:11:08,260
That's about two hundred sixteen hours a

335
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:08,580
month.

336
00:11:08,760 --> 00:11:11,834
Subtract four hundred dollars of work related

337
00:11:11,834 --> 00:11:14,157
costs, and your real wage is about

338
00:11:14,157 --> 00:11:17,026
sixteen dollars and seventy cents an hour.

339
00:11:17,377 --> 00:11:18,874
Now look at a purchase.

340
00:11:19,054 --> 00:11:21,553
A one thousand dollar phone upgrade costs

341
00:11:21,553 --> 00:11:23,443
about sixty hours of your life.

342
00:11:23,623 --> 00:11:25,187
A forty dollar dinner,

343
00:11:25,187 --> 00:11:27,106
about two and a half hours.

344
00:11:27,286 --> 00:11:29,794
Sometimes that's absolutely worth it.

345
00:11:29,974 --> 00:11:31,274
Sometimes it's not.

346
00:11:31,454 --> 00:11:33,765
Seeing price in hours makes the choice

347
00:11:33,765 --> 00:11:34,252
clearer.

348
00:11:34,602 --> 00:11:35,600
Quick pause.

349
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:38,251
If the idea of work becoming optional

350
00:11:38,251 --> 00:11:39,521
sounds good to you,

351
00:11:39,521 --> 00:11:41,190
subscribe to the channel.

352
00:11:41,370 --> 00:11:43,262
It's free, and it helps me keep

353
00:11:43,262 --> 00:11:44,726
making videos like this.

354
00:11:44,906 --> 00:11:46,810
Now, the twelve step plan.

355
00:11:47,160 --> 00:11:48,088
Angle three.

356
00:11:48,268 --> 00:11:49,011
The playbook.

357
00:11:49,191 --> 00:11:51,621
Twelve steps to retire early on a

358
00:11:51,621 --> 00:11:52,651
normal income.

359
00:11:53,002 --> 00:11:53,721
Step one.

360
00:11:53,901 --> 00:11:55,504
Calculate your real spending.

361
00:11:55,854 --> 00:11:58,130
Look at the last twelve months of

362
00:11:58,130 --> 00:12:01,786
spending, including irregular costs like car repairs,

363
00:12:01,786 --> 00:12:02,959
gifts and travel.

364
00:12:03,139 --> 00:12:05,395
That yearly total is the foundation of

365
00:12:05,395 --> 00:12:06,285
your FI number.

366
00:12:06,465 --> 00:12:09,116
Many people underestimate it by twenty percent

367
00:12:09,116 --> 00:12:09,577
or more.

368
00:12:09,927 --> 00:12:12,081
Then multiply by twenty five,

369
00:12:12,081 --> 00:12:14,385
or by twenty eight to thirty if

370
00:12:14,385 --> 00:12:16,614
you plan to retire very early.

371
00:12:16,794 --> 00:12:18,141
That's your first estimate.

372
00:12:18,321 --> 00:12:20,491
It will change as your life changes,

373
00:12:20,491 --> 00:12:21,455
and that's okay.

374
00:12:21,806 --> 00:12:22,793
Step two.

375
00:12:22,973 --> 00:12:24,493
Find your savings rate.

376
00:12:24,844 --> 00:12:27,304
Add up everything you save and invest

377
00:12:27,304 --> 00:12:27,968
in a year,

378
00:12:27,968 --> 00:12:31,891
including retirement contributions and your employer match.

379
00:12:32,071 --> 00:12:33,958
Divide by your take home pay plus

380
00:12:33,958 --> 00:12:35,101
those contributions.

381
00:12:35,281 --> 00:12:36,686
That's your savings rate.

382
00:12:36,866 --> 00:12:39,652
Many Americans save in the single digits.

383
00:12:40,003 --> 00:12:41,094
Now pick a target.

384
00:12:41,274 --> 00:12:42,992
If you're at five percent,

385
00:12:42,992 --> 00:12:44,711
aim for fifteen this year.

386
00:12:44,891 --> 00:12:46,431
If you're at fifteen,

387
00:12:46,431 --> 00:12:47,898
aim for twenty five.

388
00:12:48,078 --> 00:12:50,272
Each step cuts years off your timeline.

389
00:12:50,622 --> 00:12:51,516
Step three.

390
00:12:51,696 --> 00:12:53,507
Cut the big three first.

391
00:12:53,857 --> 00:12:57,361
Early retirees almost always have modest housing

392
00:12:57,361 --> 00:12:59,186
and transportation costs.

393
00:12:59,366 --> 00:13:01,645
A smaller home or a cheaper area,

394
00:13:01,645 --> 00:13:04,132
one reliable used car instead of two

395
00:13:04,132 --> 00:13:06,412
new ones, and cooking at home can

396
00:13:06,412 --> 00:13:08,691
raise your savings rate by ten to

397
00:13:08,691 --> 00:13:10,418
twenty percentage points.

398
00:13:10,769 --> 00:13:12,090
Small cuts help too,

399
00:13:12,090 --> 00:13:14,205
but they can't do the job alone.

400
00:13:14,385 --> 00:13:16,381
Cutting a few subscriptions might save a

401
00:13:16,381 --> 00:13:17,729
few hundred dollars a year.

402
00:13:17,909 --> 00:13:20,614
A cheaper apartment might save several thousand.

403
00:13:20,794 --> 00:13:23,093
Focus where the big money is.

404
00:13:23,443 --> 00:13:24,360
Step four.

405
00:13:24,540 --> 00:13:26,038
Kill high interest debt.

406
00:13:26,388 --> 00:13:28,735
You can't build wealth quickly while paying

407
00:13:28,735 --> 00:13:30,045
twenty percent interest.

408
00:13:30,225 --> 00:13:32,252
Pay off credit cards and other high

409
00:13:32,252 --> 00:13:33,179
rate debt first,

410
00:13:33,179 --> 00:13:35,496
while still getting your employer match.

411
00:13:35,676 --> 00:13:38,346
Then roll those payments straight into investing.

412
00:13:38,696 --> 00:13:39,877
Lower rate debts,

413
00:13:39,877 --> 00:13:42,449
like some mortgages or student loans,

414
00:13:42,449 --> 00:13:43,839
are a judgment call.

415
00:13:44,019 --> 00:13:46,307
Many early retirees keep paying them on

416
00:13:46,307 --> 00:13:48,361
schedule and invest the difference.

417
00:13:48,541 --> 00:13:50,759
Others prefer to be debt free before

418
00:13:50,759 --> 00:13:51,745
they leave work.

419
00:13:51,925 --> 00:13:52,808
Both can work.

420
00:13:53,158 --> 00:13:54,052
Step five.

421
00:13:54,232 --> 00:13:56,484
Build a strong emergency fund.

422
00:13:56,834 --> 00:13:59,284
Keep three to six months of expenses

423
00:13:59,284 --> 00:14:00,781
in high yield savings.

424
00:14:00,961 --> 00:14:02,500
With a high savings rate,

425
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:05,085
a lower monthly spending number makes this

426
00:14:05,085 --> 00:14:06,069
easier to reach.

427
00:14:06,249 --> 00:14:09,059
It protects your investments from being sold

428
00:14:09,059 --> 00:14:09,953
at a bad time.

429
00:14:10,303 --> 00:14:12,424
As you approach early retirement,

430
00:14:12,424 --> 00:14:14,932
many people build a larger cash buffer,

431
00:14:14,932 --> 00:14:17,440
sometimes one to two years of expenses,

432
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:20,269
to avoid selling investments during a market

433
00:14:20,269 --> 00:14:22,133
downturn early in retirement.

434
00:14:22,483 --> 00:14:23,574
Step six.

435
00:14:23,754 --> 00:14:25,658
Max your tax advantages.

436
00:14:26,008 --> 00:14:27,165
Use your 401k,

437
00:14:27,165 --> 00:14:30,143
an IRA, and a health savings account

438
00:14:30,143 --> 00:14:31,383
if you qualify.

439
00:14:31,563 --> 00:14:34,434
Tax advantaged accounts let more of your

440
00:14:34,434 --> 00:14:35,510
money compound.

441
00:14:35,690 --> 00:14:37,024
For early retirees,

442
00:14:37,024 --> 00:14:38,498
a mix of traditional,

443
00:14:38,498 --> 00:14:42,006
Roth and taxable accounts gives flexibility later.

444
00:14:42,357 --> 00:14:45,050
An HSA can be especially powerful.

445
00:14:45,230 --> 00:14:47,772
Contributions can be tax deductible,

446
00:14:47,772 --> 00:14:49,396
growth can be tax free,

447
00:14:49,396 --> 00:14:52,715
and withdrawals for qualified medical costs can

448
00:14:52,715 --> 00:14:53,845
be tax free too.

449
00:14:54,025 --> 00:14:57,145
Some early retirees pay medical costs out

450
00:14:57,145 --> 00:14:59,427
of pocket and let the HSA grow

451
00:14:59,427 --> 00:15:00,341
for decades.

452
00:15:00,691 --> 00:15:01,736
Step seven.

453
00:15:01,916 --> 00:15:04,609
Invest in low cost index funds.

454
00:15:04,959 --> 00:15:08,029
Most early retirees use broad index funds

455
00:15:08,029 --> 00:15:09,452
with very low fees.

456
00:15:09,632 --> 00:15:10,723
A total U.

457
00:15:10,903 --> 00:15:11,205
S.

458
00:15:11,385 --> 00:15:12,726
stock market fund,

459
00:15:12,726 --> 00:15:14,366
an international fund,

460
00:15:14,366 --> 00:15:16,006
and maybe a bond fund.

461
00:15:16,186 --> 00:15:17,869
Or a single target date fund.

462
00:15:18,049 --> 00:15:20,468
Low fees matter even more over a

463
00:15:20,468 --> 00:15:21,451
long horizon.

464
00:15:21,801 --> 00:15:23,182
And then stay the course.

465
00:15:23,362 --> 00:15:24,941
Don't sell in crashes.

466
00:15:25,121 --> 00:15:26,607
Don't chase hot trends.

467
00:15:26,787 --> 00:15:29,251
The plan works because of time and

468
00:15:29,251 --> 00:15:31,571
consistency, not clever trading.

469
00:15:31,921 --> 00:15:32,768
Step eight.

470
00:15:32,948 --> 00:15:34,260
Grow your income.

471
00:15:34,610 --> 00:15:37,765
Raising income while keeping spending steady is

472
00:15:37,765 --> 00:15:40,450
the fastest way to increase your savings

473
00:15:40,450 --> 00:15:40,786
rate.

474
00:15:40,966 --> 00:15:43,078
A ten thousand dollar raise with no

475
00:15:43,078 --> 00:15:46,095
lifestyle change can add several percentage points

476
00:15:46,095 --> 00:15:48,025
to your savings rate in a single

477
00:15:48,025 --> 00:15:48,327
year.

478
00:15:48,677 --> 00:15:50,130
Side income helps too,

479
00:15:50,130 --> 00:15:52,640
especially if it builds skills you can

480
00:15:52,640 --> 00:15:54,424
use after early retirement.

481
00:15:54,604 --> 00:15:57,659
Many early retirees keep a small business

482
00:15:57,659 --> 00:16:00,416
or freelance work for both income and

483
00:16:00,416 --> 00:16:01,012
purpose.

484
00:16:01,363 --> 00:16:02,315
Step nine.

485
00:16:02,495 --> 00:16:03,598
Plan for healthcare.

486
00:16:03,948 --> 00:16:06,664
Medicare generally starts at sixty five.

487
00:16:06,844 --> 00:16:08,040
Retire before that,

488
00:16:08,040 --> 00:16:09,677
and you need another plan.

489
00:16:09,857 --> 00:16:13,724
Options include the Affordable Care Act marketplace

490
00:16:13,724 --> 00:16:15,392
at healthcare dot gov,

491
00:16:15,392 --> 00:16:17,288
a spouse's employer plan,

492
00:16:17,288 --> 00:16:19,790
or COBRA for a limited time after

493
00:16:19,790 --> 00:16:20,852
leaving a job.

494
00:16:21,202 --> 00:16:24,912
Marketplace premium subsidies are based on income,

495
00:16:24,912 --> 00:16:28,177
so early retirees with modest taxable income

496
00:16:28,177 --> 00:16:29,735
may qualify for help.

497
00:16:29,915 --> 00:16:32,910
Rules change, so check current details.

498
00:16:33,090 --> 00:16:35,784
Include healthcare clearly in your FI number.

499
00:16:35,964 --> 00:16:38,152
Underestimating it is one of the most

500
00:16:38,152 --> 00:16:39,099
common mistakes.

501
00:16:39,449 --> 00:16:40,435
Step ten.

502
00:16:40,615 --> 00:16:42,633
Plan how to access your money before

503
00:16:42,633 --> 00:16:43,866
fifty nine and a half.

504
00:16:44,217 --> 00:16:47,399
Retirement accounts generally have a ten percent

505
00:16:47,399 --> 00:16:50,449
penalty on early withdrawals before fifty nine

506
00:16:50,449 --> 00:16:51,178
and a half,

507
00:16:51,178 --> 00:16:52,239
with exceptions.

508
00:16:52,419 --> 00:16:55,043
Early retirees use several strategies.

509
00:16:55,223 --> 00:16:58,135
A taxable brokerage account has no age

510
00:16:58,135 --> 00:16:58,671
limits.

511
00:16:58,851 --> 00:17:00,520
Roth IRA contributions,

512
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:04,076
not earnings, can generally be withdrawn anytime.

513
00:17:04,426 --> 00:17:08,111
Other strategies include a Roth conversion ladder,

514
00:17:08,111 --> 00:17:11,428
which converts traditional funds to Roth over

515
00:17:11,428 --> 00:17:13,786
time, the rule of fifty five for

516
00:17:13,786 --> 00:17:16,219
some 401k plans if you leave your

517
00:17:16,219 --> 00:17:18,283
job in or after the year you

518
00:17:18,283 --> 00:17:19,462
turn fifty five,

519
00:17:19,462 --> 00:17:23,221
and substantially equal periodic payments under IRS

520
00:17:23,221 --> 00:17:23,663
rules.

521
00:17:23,843 --> 00:17:25,276
These can be complex,

522
00:17:25,276 --> 00:17:27,733
so consider professional tax advice.

523
00:17:28,083 --> 00:17:29,046
Step eleven.

524
00:17:29,226 --> 00:17:30,852
Test drive your retirement.

525
00:17:31,202 --> 00:17:32,579
Before leaving your job,

526
00:17:32,579 --> 00:17:34,991
live on your planned retirement budget for

527
00:17:34,991 --> 00:17:35,393
a year.

528
00:17:35,573 --> 00:17:37,349
Include healthcare costs.

529
00:17:37,529 --> 00:17:38,755
If it works comfortably,

530
00:17:38,755 --> 00:17:40,083
you've proven your number.

531
00:17:40,263 --> 00:17:41,184
If it doesn't,

532
00:17:41,184 --> 00:17:43,026
adjust before it's too late.

533
00:17:43,377 --> 00:17:44,630
Also test the time.

534
00:17:44,810 --> 00:17:47,265
Take a long vacation or a sabbatical

535
00:17:47,265 --> 00:17:48,015
if you can.

536
00:17:48,195 --> 00:17:50,204
Many people discover what they want to

537
00:17:50,204 --> 00:17:51,473
do with their free time,

538
00:17:51,473 --> 00:17:53,059
and what they miss about work.

539
00:17:53,410 --> 00:17:54,373
Step twelve.

540
00:17:54,553 --> 00:17:56,597
Stay flexible after you retire.

541
00:17:56,947 --> 00:17:59,284
The early years of retirement carry the

542
00:17:59,284 --> 00:17:59,884
most risk.

543
00:18:00,064 --> 00:18:02,689
If markets fall sharply right after you

544
00:18:02,689 --> 00:18:05,583
retire, withdrawing too much can do lasting

545
00:18:05,583 --> 00:18:06,055
damage.

546
00:18:06,235 --> 00:18:08,847
This is called sequence of returns risk.

547
00:18:09,027 --> 00:18:11,546
Flexibility is your best defense.

548
00:18:11,897 --> 00:18:14,219
Cut spending a little in bad years.

549
00:18:14,399 --> 00:18:16,036
Earn some part time income.

550
00:18:16,216 --> 00:18:18,793
Use your cash buffer instead of selling

551
00:18:18,793 --> 00:18:20,511
investments at low prices.

552
00:18:20,691 --> 00:18:24,074
Small adjustments can dramatically improve the odds

553
00:18:24,074 --> 00:18:25,533
that your money lasts.

554
00:18:25,883 --> 00:18:27,578
Let's walk through a sample plan.

555
00:18:27,758 --> 00:18:29,731
Sam and Jess are both thirty,

556
00:18:29,731 --> 00:18:32,589
with a combined income of seventy thousand

557
00:18:32,589 --> 00:18:33,133
dollars.

558
00:18:33,313 --> 00:18:35,893
After taxes and retirement contributions,

559
00:18:35,893 --> 00:18:38,348
they bring home about forty six hundred

560
00:18:38,348 --> 00:18:38,851
a month.

561
00:18:39,031 --> 00:18:41,671
They contribute ten percent to their 401k

562
00:18:41,671 --> 00:18:43,796
plans, with a four percent match,

563
00:18:43,796 --> 00:18:46,243
so about eight hundred dollars a month

564
00:18:46,243 --> 00:18:48,818
is invested before the paycheck arrives.

565
00:18:49,169 --> 00:18:51,503
They rent a modest two bedroom,

566
00:18:51,503 --> 00:18:53,235
share one paid off car,

567
00:18:53,235 --> 00:18:54,741
and cook most meals.

568
00:18:54,921 --> 00:18:56,948
Their spending is about thirty four hundred

569
00:18:56,948 --> 00:18:57,325
a month.

570
00:18:57,505 --> 00:19:00,297
That leaves twelve hundred a month to

571
00:19:00,297 --> 00:19:03,089
invest in IRAs and a taxable account.

572
00:19:03,269 --> 00:19:06,195
Total investing: about two thousand a month,

573
00:19:06,195 --> 00:19:08,988
roughly thirty seven percent of their take

574
00:19:08,988 --> 00:19:10,850
home pay plus contributions.

575
00:19:11,201 --> 00:19:13,722
Their yearly spending is about forty one

576
00:19:13,722 --> 00:19:16,432
thousand dollars, so twenty five times that

577
00:19:16,432 --> 00:19:18,259
is about one million dollars.

578
00:19:18,439 --> 00:19:20,834
Investing two thousand a month at five

579
00:19:20,834 --> 00:19:22,346
percent after inflation,

580
00:19:22,346 --> 00:19:24,740
and using raises to keep their savings

581
00:19:24,740 --> 00:19:27,953
growing, they could reach financial independence in

582
00:19:27,953 --> 00:19:29,214
their early fifties.

583
00:19:29,394 --> 00:19:31,722
Maybe sooner, if their income grows faster

584
00:19:31,722 --> 00:19:32,830
than their spending.

585
00:19:33,180 --> 00:19:34,584
Is that a sacrifice?

586
00:19:34,764 --> 00:19:35,879
In some ways.

587
00:19:36,059 --> 00:19:38,185
They don't have two new cars or

588
00:19:38,185 --> 00:19:39,008
a big house.

589
00:19:39,188 --> 00:19:41,013
But they travel once a year,

590
00:19:41,013 --> 00:19:42,317
eat out on weekends,

591
00:19:42,317 --> 00:19:43,751
and they're debt free.

592
00:19:43,931 --> 00:19:46,077
And they'll likely be free to choose

593
00:19:46,077 --> 00:19:47,865
what to do with their time ten

594
00:19:47,865 --> 00:19:50,071
or fifteen years before most of their

595
00:19:50,071 --> 00:19:50,548
friends.

596
00:19:50,899 --> 00:19:53,232
Early retirement looks different for everyone.

597
00:19:53,412 --> 00:19:56,225
Take a teacher who earns sixty thousand

598
00:19:56,225 --> 00:19:56,802
dollars.

599
00:19:56,982 --> 00:19:58,878
Through a state pension,

600
00:19:58,878 --> 00:20:00,615
a 403b and a Roth IRA,

601
00:20:00,615 --> 00:20:02,432
and by living modestly,

602
00:20:02,432 --> 00:20:04,643
she may be able to retire in

603
00:20:04,643 --> 00:20:07,723
her mid fifties with a pension covering

604
00:20:07,723 --> 00:20:09,382
part of her expenses.

605
00:20:09,562 --> 00:20:12,216
Pensions can be a powerful early retirement

606
00:20:12,216 --> 00:20:14,438
tool, depending on the plan's rules.

607
00:20:14,788 --> 00:20:16,927
Or a nurse who saves forty percent

608
00:20:16,927 --> 00:20:19,444
of a seventy five thousand dollar salary

609
00:20:19,444 --> 00:20:22,086
while working night shifts and living with

610
00:20:22,086 --> 00:20:23,785
a roommate for a few years.

611
00:20:23,965 --> 00:20:25,396
By her mid forties,

612
00:20:25,396 --> 00:20:27,128
she may reach coast FI,

613
00:20:27,128 --> 00:20:29,612
cut to part time work she enjoys,

614
00:20:29,612 --> 00:20:32,925
and let her portfolio grow until traditional

615
00:20:32,925 --> 00:20:34,054
retirement age.

616
00:20:34,405 --> 00:20:36,703
Or an electrician who builds a small

617
00:20:36,703 --> 00:20:37,597
side business.

618
00:20:37,777 --> 00:20:39,466
Between his union pension,

619
00:20:39,466 --> 00:20:42,259
his savings and part time contracting work,

620
00:20:42,259 --> 00:20:44,533
he might stop full time work around

621
00:20:44,533 --> 00:20:46,677
fifty, and keep doing the jobs he

622
00:20:46,677 --> 00:20:48,366
enjoys a few days a month.

623
00:20:48,546 --> 00:20:50,357
That's barista FI in action.

624
00:20:50,707 --> 00:20:52,886
None of these paths requires a six

625
00:20:52,886 --> 00:20:53,783
figure salary.

626
00:20:53,963 --> 00:20:56,093
All of them require a high savings

627
00:20:56,093 --> 00:20:58,661
rate, patience, and a plan for healthcare

628
00:20:58,661 --> 00:20:59,350
and income.

629
00:20:59,530 --> 00:21:01,323
These are illustrations,

630
00:21:01,323 --> 00:21:02,293
not promises.

631
00:21:02,473 --> 00:21:04,977
But the principles behind them are available

632
00:21:04,977 --> 00:21:05,945
to almost anyone.

633
00:21:06,295 --> 00:21:08,903
What these stories share is a combination

634
00:21:08,903 --> 00:21:09,476
of tools.

635
00:21:09,656 --> 00:21:12,460
A pension or part time work covers

636
00:21:12,460 --> 00:21:13,615
some expenses.

637
00:21:13,795 --> 00:21:15,455
Investments cover the rest.

638
00:21:15,635 --> 00:21:17,945
Social Security arrives later.

639
00:21:18,125 --> 00:21:21,225
Early retirement on a normal income usually

640
00:21:21,225 --> 00:21:22,955
isn't one big portfolio.

641
00:21:23,135 --> 00:21:25,711
It's several income sources fitted together,

642
00:21:25,711 --> 00:21:27,175
with a plan for the gaps.

643
00:21:27,526 --> 00:21:29,372
Let's be clear about the risks.

644
00:21:29,552 --> 00:21:32,336
Market risk: a major crash right after

645
00:21:32,336 --> 00:21:33,801
you retire can hurt.

646
00:21:33,981 --> 00:21:37,216
Inflation risk: prices can rise faster than

647
00:21:37,216 --> 00:21:37,893
expected.

648
00:21:38,073 --> 00:21:40,916
Healthcare risk: costs before Medicare can be

649
00:21:40,916 --> 00:21:42,369
high and unpredictable.

650
00:21:42,549 --> 00:21:43,799
And longevity risk:

651
00:21:43,799 --> 00:21:45,970
your money may need to last fifty

652
00:21:45,970 --> 00:21:46,891
years or more.

653
00:21:47,242 --> 00:21:49,030
Policy risk matters too.

654
00:21:49,210 --> 00:21:52,752
Tax laws, healthcare rules and retirement account

655
00:21:52,752 --> 00:21:53,981
rules can change.

656
00:21:54,161 --> 00:21:57,112
That's why many early retirees build in

657
00:21:57,112 --> 00:21:59,609
margins: a lower withdrawal rate,

658
00:21:59,609 --> 00:22:00,668
a cash buffer,

659
00:22:00,668 --> 00:22:03,090
some flexibility to earn income,

660
00:22:03,090 --> 00:22:05,133
and a mix of account types.

661
00:22:05,483 --> 00:22:08,060
None of these risks means early retirement

662
00:22:08,060 --> 00:22:08,919
is impossible.

663
00:22:09,099 --> 00:22:10,635
It means plan carefully,

664
00:22:10,635 --> 00:22:11,787
keep options open,

665
00:22:11,787 --> 00:22:14,155
and don't cut your safety margins too

666
00:22:14,155 --> 00:22:14,475
thin.

667
00:22:14,655 --> 00:22:16,599
A plan that reaches FI two years

668
00:22:16,599 --> 00:22:18,786
later but with a stronger cushion is

669
00:22:18,786 --> 00:22:20,123
often the better plan.

670
00:22:20,473 --> 00:22:22,933
Of course, real life won't follow a

671
00:22:22,933 --> 00:22:24,339
spreadsheet exactly.

672
00:22:24,519 --> 00:22:26,864
Raises will come at different times,

673
00:22:26,864 --> 00:22:28,622
markets will rise and fall,

674
00:22:28,622 --> 00:22:30,707
and expenses will surprise them.

675
00:22:30,887 --> 00:22:31,998
But the structure,

676
00:22:31,998 --> 00:22:33,233
a high savings rate,

677
00:22:33,233 --> 00:22:35,516
low fixed costs and steady investing,

678
00:22:35,516 --> 00:22:37,862
gives them a strong chance of reaching

679
00:22:37,862 --> 00:22:40,825
freedom years ahead of the traditional schedule.

680
00:22:41,175 --> 00:22:43,087
Reaching your number is only half the

681
00:22:43,087 --> 00:22:43,346
plan.

682
00:22:43,526 --> 00:22:45,801
The other half is withdrawing it wisely.

683
00:22:45,981 --> 00:22:47,849
A common approach is to hold a

684
00:22:47,849 --> 00:22:49,780
cash buffer of one to two years

685
00:22:49,780 --> 00:22:52,457
of spending, and refill it from investments

686
00:22:52,457 --> 00:22:54,201
when markets are doing well.

687
00:22:54,381 --> 00:22:55,149
In bad years,

688
00:22:55,149 --> 00:22:57,157
you spend from the buffer and give

689
00:22:57,157 --> 00:22:59,106
your investments time to recover.

690
00:22:59,457 --> 00:23:02,870
Another approach is a flexible withdrawal rule.

691
00:23:03,050 --> 00:23:05,607
Start around three and a half percent,

692
00:23:05,607 --> 00:23:06,347
and adjust.

693
00:23:06,527 --> 00:23:07,578
In strong years,

694
00:23:07,578 --> 00:23:08,826
take a small raise.

695
00:23:09,006 --> 00:23:10,042
In weak years,

696
00:23:10,042 --> 00:23:11,374
cut back slightly.

697
00:23:11,554 --> 00:23:15,356
Research on flexible withdrawal strategies suggests even

698
00:23:15,356 --> 00:23:18,546
modest adjustments can greatly improve the odds

699
00:23:18,546 --> 00:23:19,972
of the money lasting.

700
00:23:20,322 --> 00:23:21,761
And taxes matter.

701
00:23:21,941 --> 00:23:25,281
Withdrawals from traditional accounts are usually taxed

702
00:23:25,281 --> 00:23:25,889
as income.

703
00:23:26,069 --> 00:23:28,238
Roth withdrawals can be tax free if

704
00:23:28,238 --> 00:23:29,354
the rules are met.

705
00:23:29,534 --> 00:23:32,285
Long term gains in taxable accounts are

706
00:23:32,285 --> 00:23:34,190
often taxed at lower rates.

707
00:23:34,370 --> 00:23:36,562
With low income in early retirement,

708
00:23:36,562 --> 00:23:38,633
some people pay very little tax by

709
00:23:38,633 --> 00:23:41,313
drawing carefully from each type of account.

710
00:23:41,663 --> 00:23:43,673
This is one area where a few

711
00:23:43,673 --> 00:23:46,329
hours with a tax professional can pay

712
00:23:46,329 --> 00:23:48,267
for itself many times over,

713
00:23:48,267 --> 00:23:50,995
especially when combining withdrawals,

714
00:23:50,995 --> 00:23:54,010
Roth conversions and healthcare subsidies,

715
00:23:54,010 --> 00:23:55,374
which all interact.

716
00:23:55,724 --> 00:23:57,866
Your FI number depends on your spending,

717
00:23:57,866 --> 00:23:59,420
and where you live has a huge

718
00:23:59,420 --> 00:24:00,437
effect on spending.

719
00:24:00,617 --> 00:24:04,244
Housing costs can vary enormously between cities

720
00:24:04,244 --> 00:24:05,075
and states.

721
00:24:05,255 --> 00:24:07,566
A home that costs five hundred thousand

722
00:24:07,566 --> 00:24:09,580
dollars in one area might cost two

723
00:24:09,580 --> 00:24:11,595
hundred fifty thousand in another.

724
00:24:11,945 --> 00:24:14,649
Some early retirees move to lower cost

725
00:24:14,649 --> 00:24:16,712
areas when they stop working,

726
00:24:16,712 --> 00:24:20,199
a strategy sometimes called geographic arbitrage.

727
00:24:20,379 --> 00:24:22,925
If your spending falls from fifty thousand

728
00:24:22,925 --> 00:24:24,441
to forty thousand a year,

729
00:24:24,441 --> 00:24:26,441
your FI number falls by about two

730
00:24:26,441 --> 00:24:28,321
hundred fifty thousand dollars.

731
00:24:28,501 --> 00:24:30,695
That could mean retiring years earlier.

732
00:24:31,045 --> 00:24:32,322
It's not for everyone.

733
00:24:32,502 --> 00:24:35,497
Family, friends and community matter a lot.

734
00:24:35,677 --> 00:24:37,108
But even a smaller move,

735
00:24:37,108 --> 00:24:39,492
like a cheaper neighborhood or a smaller

736
00:24:39,492 --> 00:24:41,459
home, can make a real difference.

737
00:24:41,809 --> 00:24:44,529
For couples, early retirement is a shared

738
00:24:44,529 --> 00:24:45,059
project.

739
00:24:45,239 --> 00:24:47,187
Both people need to agree on the

740
00:24:47,187 --> 00:24:49,500
goal, the trade offs and the timeline.

741
00:24:49,680 --> 00:24:52,292
Disagreements about spending are common.

742
00:24:52,472 --> 00:24:54,727
Talking early and often about what you

743
00:24:54,727 --> 00:24:56,745
each value helps avoid resentment.

744
00:24:57,095 --> 00:24:59,370
Some couples choose a staggered path.

745
00:24:59,550 --> 00:25:02,149
One partner leaves full time work first,

746
00:25:02,149 --> 00:25:04,423
maybe to care for children or start

747
00:25:04,423 --> 00:25:07,086
a business, while the other keeps working

748
00:25:07,086 --> 00:25:09,035
and provides health insurance.

749
00:25:09,215 --> 00:25:11,218
That can reduce risk and make the

750
00:25:11,218 --> 00:25:12,431
transition smoother.

751
00:25:12,782 --> 00:25:14,722
If you're within five years of your

752
00:25:14,722 --> 00:25:17,716
number, focus shifts from accumulation to preparation.

753
00:25:17,896 --> 00:25:19,359
Build your cash buffer.

754
00:25:19,539 --> 00:25:20,793
Plan healthcare.

755
00:25:20,973 --> 00:25:23,116
Map out which accounts you'll draw from

756
00:25:23,116 --> 00:25:23,445
first.

757
00:25:23,625 --> 00:25:25,831
And test drive your retirement budget,

758
00:25:25,831 --> 00:25:26,934
if you haven't yet.

759
00:25:27,285 --> 00:25:29,595
Also think about your investment mix.

760
00:25:29,775 --> 00:25:32,535
Some people reduce risk slightly as they

761
00:25:32,535 --> 00:25:34,329
approach early retirement,

762
00:25:34,329 --> 00:25:36,606
because a big drop just before or

763
00:25:36,606 --> 00:25:39,089
after they stop working can hurt the

764
00:25:39,089 --> 00:25:39,434
most.

765
00:25:39,614 --> 00:25:41,619
A balanced mix and a cash buffer

766
00:25:41,619 --> 00:25:42,935
can soften that risk.

767
00:25:43,285 --> 00:25:44,805
Here's what to do this week.

768
00:25:44,985 --> 00:25:46,777
Day one: add up your last twelve

769
00:25:46,777 --> 00:25:47,842
months of spending.

770
00:25:48,022 --> 00:25:50,569
Day two: multiply by twenty five,

771
00:25:50,569 --> 00:25:52,654
and write that number down.

772
00:25:52,834 --> 00:25:55,783
Day three: calculate your current savings rate.

773
00:25:56,133 --> 00:25:58,323
Day four: look at the savings rate

774
00:25:58,323 --> 00:26:00,707
table and find your current timeline.

775
00:26:00,887 --> 00:26:03,432
Day five: pick one big three change

776
00:26:03,432 --> 00:26:06,122
that would raise your savings rate by

777
00:26:06,122 --> 00:26:07,795
five percentage points.

778
00:26:07,975 --> 00:26:11,364
Day six: increase your retirement contribution by

779
00:26:11,364 --> 00:26:12,816
at least one percent.

780
00:26:12,996 --> 00:26:14,943
Day seven: write down what you'd do

781
00:26:14,943 --> 00:26:17,002
with your time if work were optional.

782
00:26:17,182 --> 00:26:17,994
That's your why.

783
00:26:18,345 --> 00:26:20,430
Let's look at the most common mistakes,

784
00:26:20,430 --> 00:26:21,607
so you can avoid them.

785
00:26:21,787 --> 00:26:24,422
Mistake one: underestimating spending.

786
00:26:24,602 --> 00:26:27,872
People often forget irregular costs like roof

787
00:26:27,872 --> 00:26:30,633
repairs, new cars every ten or fifteen

788
00:26:30,633 --> 00:26:32,014
years, dental work,

789
00:26:32,014 --> 00:26:33,322
or helping family.

790
00:26:33,502 --> 00:26:35,742
Build these into your yearly number.

791
00:26:36,093 --> 00:26:38,240
Mistake two: forgetting healthcare.

792
00:26:38,420 --> 00:26:41,837
Before Medicare, premiums and deductibles can be

793
00:26:41,837 --> 00:26:43,831
thousands of dollars a year.

794
00:26:44,011 --> 00:26:46,602
Price your real options before you quit,

795
00:26:46,602 --> 00:26:47,250
not after.

796
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:50,490
Mistake three: retiring right at the edge.

797
00:26:50,670 --> 00:26:53,024
If your plan only works with perfect

798
00:26:53,024 --> 00:26:54,659
returns and no surprises,

799
00:26:54,659 --> 00:26:55,639
it's too tight.

800
00:26:55,819 --> 00:26:57,221
A margin of safety,

801
00:26:57,221 --> 00:26:59,581
a little extra saved or a little

802
00:26:59,581 --> 00:27:01,351
flexibility in spending,

803
00:27:01,351 --> 00:27:03,343
protects you from bad luck.

804
00:27:03,693 --> 00:27:07,535
Mistake four: losing identity and community.

805
00:27:07,715 --> 00:27:10,092
Work gives many people structure,

806
00:27:10,092 --> 00:27:12,325
friends and a sense of purpose.

807
00:27:12,505 --> 00:27:15,860
Early retirees who thrive usually replace those

808
00:27:15,860 --> 00:27:18,358
things deliberately, with projects,

809
00:27:18,358 --> 00:27:21,142
groups, volunteering or part time work.

810
00:27:21,493 --> 00:27:22,773
And mistake five:

811
00:27:22,773 --> 00:27:24,128
going too extreme.

812
00:27:24,308 --> 00:27:26,507
Some people cut spending so hard they

813
00:27:26,507 --> 00:27:28,290
burn out and abandon the plan.

814
00:27:28,470 --> 00:27:30,979
A sustainable savings rate you can keep

815
00:27:30,979 --> 00:27:33,424
for fifteen years beats an extreme one

816
00:27:33,424 --> 00:27:34,647
you quit after two.

817
00:27:34,997 --> 00:27:37,813
So what do early retirees actually do

818
00:27:37,813 --> 00:27:38,421
all day?

819
00:27:38,601 --> 00:27:41,705
Surveys and interviews with people who've reached

820
00:27:41,705 --> 00:27:44,302
financial independence show a wide range.

821
00:27:44,482 --> 00:27:46,148
Many travel slowly,

822
00:27:46,148 --> 00:27:48,602
spend more time with family,

823
00:27:48,602 --> 00:27:50,443
exercise, cook, read,

824
00:27:50,443 --> 00:27:52,284
and learn new skills.

825
00:27:52,464 --> 00:27:54,727
Many also keep earning some money,

826
00:27:54,727 --> 00:27:57,189
often from work they find meaningful.

827
00:27:57,540 --> 00:27:58,813
What changes most,

828
00:27:58,813 --> 00:27:59,874
they often say,

829
00:27:59,874 --> 00:28:01,359
isn't the activities.

830
00:28:01,539 --> 00:28:02,549
It's the feeling.

831
00:28:02,729 --> 00:28:05,121
Not needing a paycheck changes how you

832
00:28:05,121 --> 00:28:07,513
approach work, relationships and time.

833
00:28:07,693 --> 00:28:09,303
You say yes to the things you

834
00:28:09,303 --> 00:28:10,970
value and no to the things you

835
00:28:10,970 --> 00:28:11,303
don't.

836
00:28:11,483 --> 00:28:13,538
That freedom is the real goal.

837
00:28:13,888 --> 00:28:14,910
Objection one.

838
00:28:15,090 --> 00:28:15,984
I'd be bored.

839
00:28:16,164 --> 00:28:18,258
Some early retirees are,

840
00:28:18,258 --> 00:28:19,043
at first.

841
00:28:19,223 --> 00:28:20,897
But many find more purpose,

842
00:28:20,897 --> 00:28:23,252
not less, once they choose their work.

843
00:28:23,432 --> 00:28:25,398
The key is planning what you're retiring

844
00:28:25,398 --> 00:28:25,545
to.

845
00:28:25,725 --> 00:28:28,129
Volunteering, learning, creating,

846
00:28:28,129 --> 00:28:29,441
caring for family,

847
00:28:29,441 --> 00:28:31,553
or meaningful part time work.

848
00:28:31,903 --> 00:28:33,111
Objection two.

849
00:28:33,291 --> 00:28:35,241
It's only for people without kids.

850
00:28:35,421 --> 00:28:37,054
Many families pursue FI,

851
00:28:37,054 --> 00:28:39,912
though children add costs and usually slow

852
00:28:39,912 --> 00:28:40,797
the timeline.

853
00:28:40,977 --> 00:28:44,635
Families often focus on housing and transportation

854
00:28:44,635 --> 00:28:47,781
savings, use tax advantaged accounts fully,

855
00:28:47,781 --> 00:28:50,123
and aim for a slightly later but

856
00:28:50,123 --> 00:28:51,879
still early finish line.

857
00:28:52,228 --> 00:28:53,297
Objection three.

858
00:28:53,477 --> 00:28:54,649
It sounds miserable.

859
00:28:54,829 --> 00:28:56,037
It doesn't have to be.

860
00:28:56,217 --> 00:28:58,997
Saving thirty or forty percent while living

861
00:28:58,997 --> 00:29:01,261
well is possible when the big three

862
00:29:01,261 --> 00:29:02,231
are controlled.

863
00:29:02,411 --> 00:29:04,551
And even if you never retire early,

864
00:29:04,551 --> 00:29:06,935
a high savings rate gives you security,

865
00:29:06,935 --> 00:29:09,197
freedom and lower stress for the rest

866
00:29:09,197 --> 00:29:09,992
of your life.

867
00:29:10,342 --> 00:29:11,514
Objection four.

868
00:29:11,694 --> 00:29:14,078
What if the market crashes right after

869
00:29:14,078 --> 00:29:14,643
I retire?

870
00:29:14,823 --> 00:29:17,130
That's the sequence of returns risk we

871
00:29:17,130 --> 00:29:17,737
discussed.

872
00:29:17,917 --> 00:29:18,805
A cash buffer,

873
00:29:18,805 --> 00:29:20,771
a conservative withdrawal rate,

874
00:29:20,771 --> 00:29:22,991
and willingness to earn a little or

875
00:29:22,991 --> 00:29:25,020
spend a little less in bad years

876
00:29:25,020 --> 00:29:26,416
are the main defenses.

877
00:29:26,596 --> 00:29:29,758
Historical studies suggest plans with these safeguards

878
00:29:29,758 --> 00:29:31,983
have held up through severe downturns.

879
00:29:32,333 --> 00:29:33,633
And objection five.

880
00:29:33,813 --> 00:29:35,160
I love my job.

881
00:29:35,340 --> 00:29:36,129
Wonderful.

882
00:29:36,309 --> 00:29:39,316
Financial independence doesn't require leaving it.

883
00:29:39,496 --> 00:29:41,374
It means you stay because you want

884
00:29:41,374 --> 00:29:42,921
to, not because you have to.

885
00:29:43,101 --> 00:29:45,414
Many people find they enjoy their work

886
00:29:45,414 --> 00:29:47,666
more once the pressure of needing the

887
00:29:47,666 --> 00:29:48,883
paycheck disappears.

888
00:29:49,233 --> 00:29:51,355
How do people actually go from ten

889
00:29:51,355 --> 00:29:53,540
percent to thirty or forty percent?

890
00:29:53,720 --> 00:29:55,055
Usually not all at once.

891
00:29:55,235 --> 00:29:57,383
A common path looks like this.

892
00:29:57,563 --> 00:29:59,854
Year one: track spending and get the

893
00:29:59,854 --> 00:30:02,590
full match, reaching about fifteen percent.

894
00:30:02,770 --> 00:30:04,756
Year two: pay off a car loan

895
00:30:04,756 --> 00:30:06,529
and keep driving the car,

896
00:30:06,529 --> 00:30:08,231
redirecting the payment,

897
00:30:08,231 --> 00:30:09,933
reaching twenty percent.

898
00:30:10,284 --> 00:30:12,729
Year three: move to a cheaper apartment

899
00:30:12,729 --> 00:30:14,109
or take in a roommate,

900
00:30:14,109 --> 00:30:16,367
and send the savings to investments,

901
00:30:16,367 --> 00:30:18,248
reaching twenty eight percent.

902
00:30:18,428 --> 00:30:20,457
Year four: get a raise and send

903
00:30:20,457 --> 00:30:21,898
most of it to savings,

904
00:30:21,898 --> 00:30:23,861
reaching thirty three percent.

905
00:30:24,041 --> 00:30:26,851
Year five: cut a few recurring costs

906
00:30:26,851 --> 00:30:28,412
and add side income,

907
00:30:28,412 --> 00:30:30,206
reaching forty percent.

908
00:30:30,557 --> 00:30:32,500
Notice that none of those steps is

909
00:30:32,500 --> 00:30:33,587
extreme on its own.

910
00:30:33,767 --> 00:30:35,195
Each is one decision.

911
00:30:35,375 --> 00:30:37,343
But together, over five years,

912
00:30:37,343 --> 00:30:39,509
they can cut decades off the path

913
00:30:39,509 --> 00:30:41,215
to financial independence.

914
00:30:41,395 --> 00:30:44,715
That's how ordinary incomes become early retirements.

915
00:30:45,065 --> 00:30:47,133
And the effect compounds in a second

916
00:30:47,133 --> 00:30:47,363
way.

917
00:30:47,543 --> 00:30:49,721
Every year at a higher savings rate,

918
00:30:49,721 --> 00:30:52,141
you get more comfortable living on less.

919
00:30:52,321 --> 00:30:54,372
Many people say they don't miss the

920
00:30:54,372 --> 00:30:55,428
spending they cut,

921
00:30:55,428 --> 00:30:57,889
and they're surprised how much calmer they

922
00:30:57,889 --> 00:30:59,531
feel with a growing cushion.

923
00:30:59,881 --> 00:31:01,829
Let me answer the questions I hear

924
00:31:01,829 --> 00:31:03,433
most about early retirement.

925
00:31:03,784 --> 00:31:04,643
Question one.

926
00:31:04,823 --> 00:31:07,029
Is the four percent rule still safe?

927
00:31:07,209 --> 00:31:08,893
It's a historical guideline,

928
00:31:08,893 --> 00:31:09,856
not a guarantee.

929
00:31:10,036 --> 00:31:13,403
Some researchers suggest lower starting rates for

930
00:31:13,403 --> 00:31:16,426
very long retirements or when valuations are

931
00:31:16,426 --> 00:31:16,769
high.

932
00:31:16,949 --> 00:31:19,738
Many early retirees use three to three

933
00:31:19,738 --> 00:31:21,132
and a half percent,

934
00:31:21,132 --> 00:31:24,066
plus flexibility, to improve their odds.

935
00:31:24,417 --> 00:31:25,334
Question two.

936
00:31:25,514 --> 00:31:27,116
What about Social Security?

937
00:31:27,296 --> 00:31:28,575
If you retire early,

938
00:31:28,575 --> 00:31:30,302
your benefits may be lower,

939
00:31:30,302 --> 00:31:33,116
because they're based on your highest thirty

940
00:31:33,116 --> 00:31:34,587
five years of earnings.

941
00:31:34,767 --> 00:31:37,286
But you'll likely still receive something later.

942
00:31:37,466 --> 00:31:40,433
Many early retirees treat Social Security as

943
00:31:40,433 --> 00:31:42,725
a bonus that reduces how much they

944
00:31:42,725 --> 00:31:44,815
need to withdraw later in life.

945
00:31:44,995 --> 00:31:47,232
You can check your estimate at ssa

946
00:31:47,232 --> 00:31:47,759
dot gov.

947
00:31:48,109 --> 00:31:49,037
Question three.

948
00:31:49,217 --> 00:31:51,138
Should I pay off my house before

949
00:31:51,138 --> 00:31:52,039
retiring early?

950
00:31:52,219 --> 00:31:54,080
It can lower your spending and your

951
00:31:54,080 --> 00:31:55,887
FI number, which many people like.

952
00:31:56,067 --> 00:31:57,716
But it ties up money that could

953
00:31:57,716 --> 00:31:58,355
be invested.

954
00:31:58,535 --> 00:32:00,793
Many early retirees aim to have their

955
00:32:00,793 --> 00:32:03,112
mortgage paid off around the time they

956
00:32:03,112 --> 00:32:04,699
retire, for peace of mind.

957
00:32:05,050 --> 00:32:06,025
Question four.

958
00:32:06,205 --> 00:32:08,051
Do I need a financial advisor?

959
00:32:08,231 --> 00:32:11,065
Not necessarily, but a fee only fiduciary

960
00:32:11,065 --> 00:32:13,346
or a tax professional can be very

961
00:32:13,346 --> 00:32:15,696
helpful for withdrawal strategies,

962
00:32:15,696 --> 00:32:18,599
Roth conversions and healthcare subsidies.

963
00:32:18,779 --> 00:32:21,314
Even one or two planning sessions can

964
00:32:21,314 --> 00:32:22,958
prevent costly mistakes.

965
00:32:23,308 --> 00:32:24,561
And question five.

966
00:32:24,741 --> 00:32:25,972
What if I start late?

967
00:32:26,152 --> 00:32:27,793
Even at forty or forty five,

968
00:32:27,793 --> 00:32:30,021
raising your savings rate to thirty or

969
00:32:30,021 --> 00:32:33,069
forty percent can significantly move your retirement

970
00:32:33,069 --> 00:32:33,362
date.

971
00:32:33,542 --> 00:32:35,431
You may not retire at forty five,

972
00:32:35,431 --> 00:32:38,122
but you could reach financial independence well

973
00:32:38,122 --> 00:32:40,183
before a traditional retirement age.

974
00:32:40,533 --> 00:32:41,624
Question six.

975
00:32:41,804 --> 00:32:43,596
What about the people who say early

976
00:32:43,596 --> 00:32:44,672
retirement is a myth?

977
00:32:44,852 --> 00:32:46,945
Some critics point out that many FIRE

978
00:32:46,945 --> 00:32:49,039
bloggers earn money from their blogs,

979
00:32:49,039 --> 00:32:51,133
so they never really stopped working.

980
00:32:51,313 --> 00:32:51,963
That's fair.

981
00:32:52,143 --> 00:32:54,710
But many people use the same principles

982
00:32:54,710 --> 00:32:57,080
quietly, to switch to work they love

983
00:32:57,080 --> 00:32:58,726
or cut back to part time.

984
00:32:58,906 --> 00:33:01,815
Financial independence is about options,

985
00:33:01,815 --> 00:33:02,760
not idleness.

986
00:33:03,110 --> 00:33:04,305
And question seven.

987
00:33:04,485 --> 00:33:06,471
What's the single most important number?

988
00:33:06,651 --> 00:33:07,823
Your savings rate.

989
00:33:08,003 --> 00:33:10,078
It controls how fast you build your

990
00:33:10,078 --> 00:33:11,974
portfolio and how much you need.

991
00:33:12,154 --> 00:33:13,576
Raise it, keep it high,

992
00:33:13,576 --> 00:33:14,627
and give it time.

993
00:33:14,807 --> 00:33:16,363
Everything else is detail.

994
00:33:16,713 --> 00:33:17,572
Quick recap.

995
00:33:17,752 --> 00:33:20,179
Your FI number is roughly twenty five

996
00:33:20,179 --> 00:33:21,949
times your yearly spending,

997
00:33:21,949 --> 00:33:24,114
or more if you retire very early.

998
00:33:24,294 --> 00:33:26,188
The time it takes depends mostly on

999
00:33:26,188 --> 00:33:27,162
your savings rate.

1000
00:33:27,342 --> 00:33:29,513
Ten percent takes about fifty years.

1001
00:33:29,693 --> 00:33:31,945
Fifty percent takes about seventeen.

1002
00:33:32,295 --> 00:33:33,409
The twelve steps.

1003
00:33:33,589 --> 00:33:35,273
Calculate your real spending.

1004
00:33:35,453 --> 00:33:36,846
Find your savings rate.

1005
00:33:37,026 --> 00:33:38,558
Cut the big three.

1006
00:33:38,738 --> 00:33:40,073
Kill high interest debt.

1007
00:33:40,253 --> 00:33:42,366
Build a strong emergency fund.

1008
00:33:42,546 --> 00:33:44,381
Max your tax advantages.

1009
00:33:44,561 --> 00:33:47,359
Invest in low cost index funds.

1010
00:33:47,539 --> 00:33:48,769
Grow your income.

1011
00:33:48,949 --> 00:33:50,261
Plan for healthcare.

1012
00:33:50,441 --> 00:33:52,849
Plan access before fifty nine and a

1013
00:33:52,849 --> 00:33:53,193
half.

1014
00:33:53,373 --> 00:33:55,080
Test drive your retirement.

1015
00:33:55,260 --> 00:33:57,535
And stay flexible after you retire.

1016
00:33:57,886 --> 00:33:58,954
One last thought.

1017
00:33:59,134 --> 00:34:01,286
You don't have to decide today whether

1018
00:34:01,286 --> 00:34:03,156
you want to retire at forty five,

1019
00:34:03,156 --> 00:34:04,289
fifty five or never.

1020
00:34:04,469 --> 00:34:06,427
Raising your savings rate gives you the

1021
00:34:06,427 --> 00:34:06,779
option.

1022
00:34:06,959 --> 00:34:09,078
And options, more than any number,

1023
00:34:09,078 --> 00:34:11,882
are what financial freedom really feels like.

1024
00:34:12,232 --> 00:34:14,440
This week, calculate two numbers:

1025
00:34:14,440 --> 00:34:17,317
your yearly spending and your savings rate.

1026
00:34:17,497 --> 00:34:19,645
Those two numbers tell you more about

1027
00:34:19,645 --> 00:34:21,909
your future than your salary ever will.

1028
00:34:22,089 --> 00:34:24,218
And watch my video on the complete

1029
00:34:24,218 --> 00:34:25,096
budget system.

1030
00:34:25,276 --> 00:34:27,470
It's how you raise that savings rate.

1031
00:34:27,650 --> 00:34:28,904
I'm Ryan Rover.

1032
00:34:29,084 --> 00:34:30,233
See you in the next one.
